  ],
  "text": "Technically, the inside of your gut is still the outside of your body. You are a donut. Nothing is truly inside you until it crosses the wall of that tube. It runs about nine metres, and a meal takes a full day to cross it. Chewing starts it. Enzymes in your saliva break down starch before you have even swallowed. The stomach is a chemical bath. Acid that dissolves metal, held off your own flesh by mucus. But almost nothing is absorbed there. That job belongs to the small intestine. Its wall is folded, and the folds are furred, and the fur is furred again. Unfolded, that surface would cover a tennis court. The large intestine takes what is left and reclaims the water out of it. Nothing you eat is inside you until it crosses that wall. Everything else is just passing through. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
